::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which statement is the best focused purpose for a short speech?
{
~A. I will discuss several things about school.#This is broad and does not tell the audience the exact point of the speech.
=B. I will explain how daily practice helps speakers become more confident.#Correct. This gives one clear idea that can guide the whole speech.
~C. Speaking, school, and sports are interesting.#This lists unrelated topics instead of giving one coherent focus.
~D. My speech is about anything important.#This is vague. A focused speech needs a specific direction.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which order best fits a speech explaining how to prepare for an interview?
{
~A. Arrive early, research the company, practice answers.#This starts with a later step. Preparation should begin before the day of the interview.
~B. Practice answers, arrive early, research the company.#This puts research after practice. Strong practice depends on knowing the situation first.
~C. Arrive early, practice answers, research the company.#This order is confusing because arrival happens after preparation.
=D. Research the company, practice answers, arrive early.#Correct. The steps move from preparation to final action in a logical sequence.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which outline shows a quality speech format?
{
=A. Introduction, Point 1, Point 2, Point 3, Conclusion.#Correct. This structure helps the speaker and audience follow the presentation.
~B. Point 2, conclusion, random fact, introduction.#This order does not follow an accepted speech structure.
~C. A single paragraph with no clear parts.#This makes it hard to see the speech structure and weakens organization.
~D. A list of jokes before the topic is introduced.#This does not show a clear presentation format or coherent focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which sentence uses an appropriate voice and tone for an informative classroom speech?
{
~A. You all should already know this.#This sounds dismissive and can distance the audience.
~B. This topic is boring, but I have to present it.#This weakens the speaker's credibility and tone.
=C. Today I will explain three practical ways students can prepare for a presentation.#Correct. The tone is respectful, clear, and focused on informing the audience.
~D. I guess I will say some stuff now.#This is too casual and unclear for a formal classroom presentation.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which main point best supports the focus "Public speaking practice improves confidence"?
{
=A. Rehearsing aloud helps speakers become more comfortable with their words.#Correct. This point directly supports how practice builds confidence.
~B. Some classrooms have desks in rows.#This does not connect to public speaking practice or confidence.
~C. Lunch schedules can affect student energy.#This shifts to a different topic and weakens focus.
~D. Many people enjoy watching movies.#This is unrelated to the speech focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Why are transitions important in a speech?
{
~A. They replace the need for main points.#Transitions do not replace content. They connect content.
=B. They help the audience understand how one idea leads to the next.#Correct. Transitions guide listeners through the speech structure.
~C. They let the speaker add unrelated information.#Transitions should strengthen focus, not introduce unrelated ideas.
~D. They make the conclusion unnecessary.#A conclusion is still needed to close the speech and restate the focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which plan is most coherent?
{
~A. Topic: school; points: clubs, weather, favorite songs.#These points do not support one clear focus.
~B. Topic: exercise; points: phones, homework, cafeteria food.#These ideas are unrelated to exercise.
=C. Topic: study groups; points: shared notes, peer support, regular review.#Correct. Each point supports the same topic and focus.
~D. Topic: attendance; points: sports teams, music apps, weekend plans.#These points drift away from attendance.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
A speaker labels each part of the outline and places transitions between main points. Which skill is the speaker showing?
{
~A. Avoiding all structure.#The speaker is using structure, not avoiding it.
~B. Replacing speaking with writing.#An outline supports speaking but does not replace delivery.
~C. Adding unrelated details.#Labels and transitions support organization when they connect ideas.
=D. Following a clear presentation format.#Correct. The speaker is using accepted speech parts to create quality work.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
What should a speaker do first when organizing a speech?
{
~A. Choose random transitions.#Transitions work best after the speaker knows the focus and points.
=B. Decide the central focus of the speech.#Correct. The focus guides every later decision about points and order.
~C. Write the conclusion before knowing the topic.#A conclusion should match the speech focus and main points.
~D. Add as many facts as possible.#Facts need to be selected because they support the focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which transition uses a clear and respectful tone?
{
=A. Now that we have looked at the first reason, let us examine a second reason.#Correct. The tone is clear, respectful, and helps the audience follow.
~B. Whatever, here is another thing.#This is too casual and does not explain the connection.
~C. I am moving on because I am done with that.#This focuses on the speaker instead of guiding the audience.
~D. This part might not matter, but listen anyway.#This weakens the speaker's confidence and tone.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which conclusion best closes a speech about practicing presentations?
{
~A. That is all, and I also like science.#This adds an unrelated idea and does not close the focus.
~B. I forgot some of my points, but that is okay.#This does not reinforce the speech purpose.
~C. There are many topics I could have talked about.#This is vague and does not summarize the speech.
=D. Regular practice helps speakers prepare, manage nerves, and communicate clearly.#Correct. This conclusion restates the focus and summarizes the key points.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which main point does not fit the focus "Healthy routines can improve student learning"?
{
~A. Getting enough sleep helps students concentrate.#This supports the focus because sleep connects to learning.
=B. Some students prefer blue notebooks.#Correct. Notebook color does not explain how healthy routines improve learning.
~C. Eating breakfast can support energy in class.#This supports the focus because energy affects learning.
~D. Planning study time can reduce stress.#This supports the focus because planning is part of a healthy routine.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What does a preview statement usually do in a speech introduction?
{
~A. It ends the speech.#A preview appears near the beginning, not the end.
~B. It replaces all evidence.#A preview guides the audience but does not replace support.
=C. It tells the audience the main points the speech will cover.#Correct. A preview prepares listeners for the organization of the speech.
~D. It introduces an unrelated story.#An unrelated story can distract from the focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
A speaker says, "This information may help you prepare for your next class presentation." What does this show?
{
=A. Audience-aware tone.#Correct. The speaker connects the information to the audience in a respectful way.
~B. A loss of focus.#The sentence keeps the presentation connected to the audience and topic.
~C. An unrelated conclusion.#The sentence is relevant to the topic of presentation preparation.
~D. A missing main point.#The sentence may introduce or connect content; it is not automatically missing a point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which revision improves coherence?
{
~A. Add more unrelated examples to make the speech longer.#More unrelated material makes coherence weaker.
~B. Remove transitions so the speech sounds faster.#Removing transitions can make the speech harder to follow.
~C. Change the topic every time the audience seems interested.#Changing topics breaks focus.
=D. Remove the point about cafeteria food from a speech about joining clubs.#Correct. Removing an unrelated point helps every idea support the same focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speech plan uses problem-solution organization?
{
~A. First, I will describe three favorite apps.#This is a list, not a problem-solution structure.
~B. First, I will tell a joke, then another joke.#This does not identify a problem and solution.
=C. First, I will explain why students miss deadlines, then I will present ways to plan ahead.#Correct. The first part names the problem and the second part offers solutions.
~D. First, I will compare two unrelated school rules.#Comparison is different from problem-solution organization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which item belongs in a speech outline?
{
~A. Private notes that are unrelated to the speech.#Unrelated private notes do not support the presentation format.
=B. Main points that support the speech focus.#Correct. Main points are core parts of an organized speech outline.
~C. Random words copied from a website.#Random copied words do not show quality speech planning.
~D. A conclusion that introduces a new topic.#A conclusion should close the same topic, not introduce a new one.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which introduction best prepares the audience for an organized speech?
{
=A. Today I will explain three ways planning ahead helps students manage homework.#Correct. It states the topic and previews the organized direction.
~B. I am going to talk for a while and see what happens.#This does not give a clear focus or structure.
~C. There are many things in school, and I will mention some.#This is vague and unfocused.
~D. My topic keeps changing, but I will begin anyway.#This signals weak organization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone problem would most likely weaken a classroom presentation?
{
~A. Speaking clearly and respectfully.#This supports, rather than weakens, a classroom presentation.
~B. Explaining how the topic connects to students.#This shows audience awareness.
=C. Sounding dismissive toward the audience.#Correct. A dismissive tone can reduce audience trust and attention.
~D. Using transitions to guide listeners.#Transitions improve organization and listening.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which question helps test whether a speech is focused?
{
~A. Is the speech as long as possible?#Length does not prove focus. A long speech can still be disorganized.
~B. Does the speaker use complicated words?#Difficult vocabulary is not the same as coherent focus.
~C. Does the speech include every idea the speaker knows?#Including every idea can weaken focus.
=D. Does each main point support the same central idea?#Correct. This question checks whether the speech stays coherent.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What is the strongest reason to use an outline before speaking?
{
=A. It helps the speaker arrange ideas before presenting them.#Correct. An outline supports clear organization and delivery.
~B. It guarantees the audience will agree.#Organization helps understanding, but it does not guarantee agreement.
~C. It allows the speaker to ignore transitions.#A good outline often includes transitions.
~D. It replaces the need to understand the topic.#The speaker still needs to understand the topic and purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which pair of main points best supports the focus "Reading aloud improves speaking skills"?
{
~A. Reading aloud is useful; cafeterias sell snacks.#The second point is unrelated and weakens the focus.
=B. Reading aloud improves pronunciation; reading aloud builds confidence.#Correct. Both points directly support the focus.
~C. Reading aloud is loud; phones are popular.#The phone point does not support the focus.
~D. Reading aloud happens at home; math tests are difficult.#The math test point is unrelated.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which phrase would best signal a final point in a speech?
{
~A. I forgot what comes next.#This weakens confidence and does not guide the audience.
~B. Anyway, one more thing.#This is casual and does not clearly organize the speech.
=C. Finally, the last reason practice matters is that it improves timing.#Correct. This phrase signals order and connects the final point to the focus.
~D. This next idea is random.#This tells the audience the speech is not coherent.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
A speaker's second point does not connect to the speech focus. What should the speaker do?
{
~A. Keep it because more points always improve a speech.#More points do not help if they are unrelated.
~B. Move it to the conclusion without changing it.#An unrelated idea will still weaken the conclusion.
~C. Say it faster so the audience notices less.#Speed does not solve a focus problem.
=D. Revise or replace it with a point that supports the focus.#Correct. Every main point should connect to the central idea.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_QZ_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which statement best describes coherent focus?
{
=A. All parts of the speech work together to support one clear idea.#Correct. Coherence means the audience can follow how every part connects.
~B. The speech includes as many topics as possible.#Too many topics can make the speech unfocused.
~C. The speaker never uses transitions.#Transitions often help create coherence.
~D. The conclusion introduces a new subject.#A conclusion should close the same focus, not shift topics.
}
